  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nAnother period of extended wet weather is forecast to enter the\narea Saturday night and into Sunday morning. Ascent will mostly\nbe due to a shortwave trough and ridge breakdown in a transition\nto more quasi-zonal flow for the beginning of next week.\n\nThe highest chance of convective rain will likely come early\nSunday morning or perhaps Sunday afternoon/evening with a split\nin modeling between when exactly the heaviest rain occurs. The\nmorning scenario is tied more to overrunning warm frontal\nconvection, while the later chances are convergence along the\ndeparting cold front. In this period, the highest risk of \nrainfall is generally eastern Ohio and northwest Pennsylvania. \nExact timing is a bit uncertain, but modeling seems to favor \nthat either one of these scenarios will be the one dominant one\nfor QPF, with HREF/NBM 95th- max QPF showing up to 2\"-3\" where \ntraining convection is most likely.\n\nThe main risk with these convective waves would be flash\nflooding, but ensemble agree that there generally wouldn't be in\nimmediate reflection in significant river rises regardless of\nscenario. Flood watches may be needed, should confidence\nincrease. Severe risk remains quite low with moist adiabatic \nprofiles and low DCAPE (>400). Up to 40kts of sfc-6km shear are \npossible, but most of this is aloft for now. If it becomes more \npacked in the low levels a quick spin up couldn't not be ruled \nout. \n\nContinued rain chances are forecast into Monday, Tuesday, and\nWednesday, though temperatures will be cooler and rain is\nexpected to be mostly stratiform. That being said, long duration\naccumulations have the potential to be sizable, with 90th\npercentile 48hr totals of up to 2\" to 4\". While this wouldn't\npose as much a threat for flash flooding, areal flooding and\nriver flooding chances may increase should this high-end\npotential be realized. The primary way to realize high end\npotential would be to be positioned immediately north of the\nstationary boundary with overrunning and/or on the northwest \nside of a developing coastal low. Should the stationary boundary\nbe too far south or the coastal low to far east, these threats\nwould not be realized. These scenarios will need to be \nmonitored through the weekend and into next week.",
